<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Cuarte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1094">1094</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Cuarte was fought in October 1094 outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, months after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> had captured the city. An Almoravid army sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> to recover Valencia camped on the plain of Cuarte and besieged it, but El Cid sallied out and routed it. It was the first major defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cuarte.jpg" alt="Battle of Cuarte" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lilyu; Manassas; Escarlati; derivative work by ecelan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_Cuarte.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> on 17 June 1094, the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> ordered the recruitment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> of some 4,000 light cavalry and between 4,000 and 6,000 infantry, and placed his nephew Muhammad ibn Tashfin in command. The contingents landed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a> between 16 and 18 August 1094 and marched through Málaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, joined by troops of the Almoravid governor of Granada and probably by men from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lérida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a>. In all the army numbered at most 10,000.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> repaired the walls, laid in food and gathered perhaps 4,000 to 8,000 fighters, Christian and Muslim, half of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a>. Fearing the pro-Almoravid faction in a city of about 15,000 inhabitants, he confiscated weapons and iron objects and expelled suspected sympathisers. Christian and Muslim sources alike praise his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psychological-warfare">psychological warfare</a>: he threatened the Muslims who remained, foretold his own victory and spread word that Peter I of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> were coming to his aid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> camped on the plain between Quart de Poblet and Mislata, between 3 and 6 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, around 15 September, but kept the siege passive during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>. When the fast ended on 14 October they began raiding the orchards and the suburbs outside the walls. The rumour of a relief army had already caused desertions, leaving the south and south-west of the city unsurrounded.</p><p>On 21 October El Cid led the bulk of his army out through the southern gate by night and made a wide detour behind the enemy camp. At dawn a smaller body of cavalry rode out of the western gate in what looked like an ordinary skirmish, drawing off the Almoravid vanguard. The main force then fell on the camp from the rear, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>, believing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> had arrived, fled in disorder. By midday the camp and its spoils were taken.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> gained large amounts of riches, horses and weapons, several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> began to pay him tribute, and by 1098 he held Almenara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagunto">Murviedro</a>, present-day Sagunto. In the endowment of the new Cathedral of Santa María in 1098 he signed as princeps Rodericus Campidoctor. His principality held until his death in mid-1099, and his wife Jimena defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iii-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer III of Barcelona</a> until May 1102, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> ordered its evacuation and the city passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Cuarte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He defeated Almoravid relief armies at <b>Cuarte</b> in 1094 and Bairén in 1097, burnt Ibn Jahhaf alive and turned the great mosque into a cathedral under Bishop Jerome of Périgord.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-valencia">Lordship of Valencia</a></li></ul></section>
